    "title": "The Kevin Shirley Sessions: Recording Under Label Pressure",
    "content": "The *Falling into Infinity* sessions at BearTracks Studios in 1997 unfolded under conditions radically different from the creative freedom that had defined *Awake* and *Images and Words*. Elektra Records, alarmed by Dream Theater's declining commercial trajectory, assigned producer Kevin Shirley — a veteran of mainstream rock production whose credits included Aerosmith and Journey — to guide the album toward radio-friendly accessibility.\n\n**Kevin Shirley's Approach:**\nShirley's engineering philosophy prioritized clarity and punch over the complex, room-aware sounds that had characterized earlier Dream Theater recordings. His drum production on *Falling into Infinity* was more direct and forward in the mix than anything the band had previously released — a pragmatic decision for an album that needed to compete on mainstream rock radio. The Starclassic Maple kit's natural warmth was captured cleanly, with less ambient character than fans of the band's earlier work were accustomed to.\n\n**The Desmond Child Controversy:**\nElektra also brought in external co-writers, most notably pop songwriter Desmond Child (famous for \"Livin' on a Prayer\" and \"Poison\"), to help craft more commercially viable material. Several tracks on the album carry co-writing credits from outside the band. Portnoy and Petrucci publicly discussed the label interference in subsequent years, describing the experience as a creative compromise that left the album in an awkward position between Dream Theater's identity and Elektra's commercial demands.\n\n**Microphone Setup:**\n- Kick drums: AKG D112 inside each bass drum for attack, with ambient blend for low-end body\n- Snare: Shure SM57 top, Sennheiser e604 bottom\n- Toms: Sennheiser MD421 on each drum\n- Hi-hat: AKG C451\n- Overheads: Neumann U87 condensers in spaced pair\n\n**What Worked:**\nDespite the difficult circumstances, the *Falling into Infinity* sessions produced some of Portnoy's finest playing. His restraint on \"Hollow Years\" — a quiet, melodic ballad unlike anything Dream Theater had attempted — demonstrated a different dimension of his musicianship. His work on the lengthier progressive tracks, \"Trial of Tears\" and \"Lines in the Sand,\" showed that even under label pressure, the band could not entirely suppress their ambitions.",
